\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{FitzHugh–Nagumo nullclines}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The FitzHugh–Nagumo equations, in the form of
\citet{(4.11) and (4.12), §4.2.6, p. 106}{izhikevich2007dynamical},
are
##{\dot V=V(a-V)(V-1)-w+I,}
##{\dot w=bV-cw.}
The state is the pair #{(V,w)}. Figure 4.20 of that source takes
#{I=0}, #{b=0.01}, #{c=0.02}, and #{a=0.1} or #{a=-0.1}. The voltage
[nullcline](fnip-0014) and the #{w}-nullcline are then
##{w=V(a-V)(V-1),}
##{w=(b/c)V=V/2.}
\citet{p. 107}{izhikevich2007dynamical}
prints #{w=V(a-V)(V-1)+I} and #{w=(b/c)V}. The values #{I=0} and
#{b/c=1/2}, from the caption of Figure 4.20, give the two equations
shown. Setting the right-hand sides
equal gives #{V=0} or
##{V^{2}-(a+1)V+(a+\tfrac12)=0.}
The discriminant of the quadratic is #{a^{2}-2a-1}. It is negative
when #{a=0.1} and when #{a=-0.1},
so the origin is the only [equilibrium](fnip-0014). The slope of the
cubic at #{V=0} is #{-a}.
\citet{Figure 4.20, pp. 106–107}{izhikevich2007dynamical}
draws the intersection on the left branch of the cubic in the first
case and on the middle branch in the second.}

